  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 100 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n18z surface analysis had warm front from weak low over southeast \nMT into eastern CO. Ahead of it, quite a bit of cloud cover \npersists. Behind it, clearing skies per 0.5km visible satellite \nloop. These features in association with shortwave trough moving \nacross eastern MT/western ND per water vapour loop. 90kt streak \noverhead. Effects of these features the main short term forecast \nconcern.\n\nLater this afternoon/evening, early Fall solar insolation should \nerase MLCIN from the 18z KUDX sounding with surface temperatures \naround 80F needed. ~1KJ/kg MLCAPE will be available along the warm\nfront with 40kts of effective shear under jet streak. Convection \nshould develop after 21z per most CAMs with isolated strong/severe\nthunderstorms possible creating marginally severe hail/wind \ngusts. Activity will move east this evening with clearing skies \nover the CWA overnight. Temperatures will be near guidance.\n\nShortwave ridging Thursday will bring dry weather for most of the\nCWA, but aforementioned warm front gets hung up over the SD/NE \nborder which may be the focus for a few shra/TS. Stronger wave \narrives Friday with increasing PoPs, especially east of the Black \nHills where best low level moisture return/buoyancy forecast. Dry \nSaturday and most of Sunday. Temperatures will be 5-10F above \nnormal through the weekend. Then, ensemble guidance portrays a \nstrong upper trough slicing across the northern Plains with \nincreasing chances of precipitation as well as cooler, more fall- \nlike temperatures.",
